[&#39;postSummary&#39;], ENT_QUOTES) . &quot;&#39;, postContent = &#39;&quot; . htmlspecialchars($_POST[&#39;postContent&#39;], ENT_QUOTES) . &quot;&#39;, UserID = &#39;&quot; . $_POST[&#39;postUserID&#39;] . &quot;&#39; WHERE PostID = &#39;$id&#39;&quot;;</code></big></small></div>\r\n\r\n<div style=\"background:#eeeeee; border:1px solid #cccccc; padding:5px 10px\"><small><big><code>$this-&gt;conn-&gt;query($sql); &nbsp; }</code></big></small></div>\r\n\r\n<div style=\"background:#eeeeee; border:1px solid #cccccc; padding:5px 10px\"><small><big><code>&nbsp; &nbsp;catch(mysqli_sql_exception $e) {</code></big></small></div>\r\n\r\n<div style=\"background:#eeeeee; border:1px solid #cccccc; padding:5px 10px\"><small><big><code>&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;$this-&gt;WriteLog(&quot;Database Error&quot;,htmlspecialchars(str_replace(&quot;&#39;&quot;,&quot; &quot;,$e-&gt;getMessage()),ENT_QUOTES),str_replace(&quot;&#39;&quot;,&quot; &quot;,&quot;Method: update() Models/Posts.php:420 - $sql&quot;)); }</code></big></small></div>\r\n\r\n<div style=\"background:#eeeeee; border:1px solid #cccccc; padding:5px 10px\"><small><big><code>}</code></big></small></div>\r\n\r\n<div style=\"background:#eeeeee; border:1px solid #cccccc; padding:5px 10px\"><small><big><code>public function delete(int $id)</code></big></small></div>\r\n\r\n<div style=\"background:#eeeeee; border:1px solid #cccccc; padding:5px 10px\"><small><big><code>{</code></big></small></div>\r\n\r\n<div style=\"background:#eeeeee; border:1px solid #cccccc; padding:5px 10px\"><small><big><code>&nbsp; &nbsp;try {</code></big></small></div>\r\n\r\n<div style=\"background:#eeeeee; border:1px solid #cccccc; padding:5px 10px\"><small><big><code>&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;$sql = &quot;DELETE FROM Posts WHERE PostID = &#39;$id&#39;&quot;; $this-&gt;conn-&gt;query($sql); &nbsp;</code></big></small></div>\r\n\r\n<div style=\"background:#eeeeee; border:1px solid #cccccc; padding:5px 10px\"><small><big><code>&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;} catch(mysqli_sql_exception $e) {</code></big></small></div>\r\n\r\n<div style=\"background:#eeeeee; border:1px solid #cccccc; padding:5px 10px\"><small><big><code>&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; $this-&gt;WriteLog(&quot;Database Error&quot;,htmlspecialchars(str_replace(&quot;&#39;&quot;,&quot; &quot;,$e-&gt;getMessage()),ENT_QUOTES),str_replace(&quot;&#39;&quot;,&quot; &quot;,&quot;Method: delete() Models/Posts.php:435 - $sql&quot;)); }</code></big></small></div>\r\n\r\n<div style=\"background:#eeeeee; border:1px solid #cccccc; padding:5px 10px\"><small><big><code>}</code></big></small></div>\r\n\r\n<div style=\"background:#eeeeee; border:1px solid #cccccc; padding:5px 10px\"><small><big><code>// End Class</code></big></small></div>\r\n\r\n<div style=\"background:#eeeeee; border:1px solid #cccccc; padding:5px 10px\"><small><big><code>}</code></big></small></div>\r\n\r\n<div style=\"background:#eeeeee; border:1px solid #cccccc; padding:5px 10px\"><small>&nbsp;</small></div>\r\n\r\n<div style=\"background:#eeeeee; border:1px solid #cccccc; padding:5px 10px\"><small><big>And that&rsquo;s it. With the methods, you&rsquo;ll create the objects you need to be filled with the real values based on the model.</big></small></div>\r\n\r\n<div style=\"background:#eeeeee; border:1px solid #cccccc; padding:5px 10px\"><small><big>View:&nbsp; &nbsp;The view is responsible to take in the data from the controller and display those values.</big></small></div>\r\n\r\n<div style=\"background:#eeeeee; border:1px solid #cccccc; padding:5px 10px\"><small><big>There are a lot of template engines for PHP, from Twig to Blade. For this MVC tutorial for PHP, we&rsquo;ll use only plain HTML to make things simple. In order to create a new view, we must create a new file called product.php under the views folder.</big></small></div>\r\n\r\n<div style=\"background:#eeeeee; border:1px solid #cccccc; padding:5px 10px\"><small><big>Based on the product attributes, we can write a simple HTML like this:</big></small></div>\r\n\r\n<div style=\"background:#eeeeee; border:1px solid #cccccc; padding:5px 10px\"><small><big><code>Simple PHP MVC My Product: getTitle(); ?&gt; getDescription(); ?&gt; getPrice(); ?&gt; getSku(); ?&gt; getImage(); ?&gt; Back to homepage &nbsp;</code></big></small></div>\r\n\r\n<div style=\"background:#eeeeee; border:1px solid #cccccc; padding:5px 10px\"><small><big>The view is now ready to get the product object ($product) and display its values.</big></small></div>\r\n\r\n<div style=\"background:#eeeeee; border:1px solid #cccccc; padding:5px 10px\"><small><big>Controller: The controller is the heart of the application logic. Is responsible for accepting the input and converting it to commands for the model or view. Let&rsquo;s create a new folder called Controllers under the app folder, and create a new controller file called ProductController.php.</big></small></div>\r\n\r\n<div style=\"background:#eeeeee; border:1px solid #cccccc; padding:5px 10px\"><small><big>Here is the content:</big></small></div>\r\n\r\n<div style=\"background:#eeeeee; border:1px solid #cccccc; padding:5px 10px\"><small><big><code>read($id); </code></big></small></div>\r\n\r\n<div style=\"background:#eeeeee; border:1px solid #cccccc; padding:5px 10px\"><small><big><code>require_once APP_ROOT . &#39;/views/product.php&#39;;</code></big></small></div>\r\n\r\n<p><small>&nbsp;</small></p>\r\n\r\n<p><small><big>Very simple, isn&rsquo;t it? Obviously, things might be more complex, we can create a parent Controller class, a view method, and other helper functions. But it&rsquo;s enough for now.</big></small></p>\r\n',	NULL,	NULL,	'25-07-2024'),
(32,	1,	8,	1,	'Authorize inbound traffic for your Linux instances',	'',	'<p>Security groups enable you to control traffic to your instance, including the kind of traffic that can reach your instance. For example, you can allow computers from only your home network to access your instance using&nbsp;SSH. If your instance is a web server, you can allow all IP addresses to access your instance using HTTP or HTTPS, so that external users can browse the content on your web server.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Your default security groups and newly created security groups include default rules that do not enable you to access your instance from the internet. For more information, see&nbsp;Default security groups&nbsp;and&nbsp;Custom security groups. To enable network access to your instance, you must allow inbound traffic to your instance. To open a port for inbound traffic, add a rule to a security group that you associated with your instance when you launched it.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>To connect to your instance, you must set up a rule to authorize&nbsp;SSH&nbsp;traffic from your computer&#39;s public IPv4 address. To allow&nbsp;SSH&nbsp;traffic from additional IP address ranges, add another rule for each range you need to authorize.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>If you&#39;ve enabled your VPC for IPv6 and launched your instance with an IPv6 address, you can connect to your instance using its IPv6 address instead of a public IPv4 address. Your local computer must have an IPv6 address and must be configured to use IPv6.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>If you need to enable network access to a Windows instance, see&nbsp;Authorizing inbound traffic for your Windows instances&nbsp;in the&nbsp;Amazon EC2 User Guide for Windows Instances.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Before you start<br />\r\n&nbsp;<br />\r\nDecide who requires access to your instance; for example, a single host or a specific network that you trust such as your local computer&#39;s public IPv4 address. The security group editor in the Amazon EC2 console can automatically detect the public IPv4 address of your local computer for you. Alternatively, you can use the search phrase &quot;what is my IP address&quot; in an internet browser, or use the following service:&nbsp;Check IP. If you are connecting through an ISP or from behind your firewall without a static IP address, you need to find out the range of IP addresses used by client computers.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Warning<br />\r\nIf you use&nbsp;0.0.0.0/0, you enable all IPv4 addresses to access your instance using&nbsp;SSH. If you use&nbsp;::/0, you enable all IPv6 addresses to access your instance. You should authorize only a specific IP address or range of addresses to access your instance.<br />\r\nDecide whether you&#39;ll support SSH access to your instances using EC2 Instance Connect. If you will not use EC2 Instance Connect, consider uninstalling it or denying the following action in your IAM policies:&nbsp;ec2-instance-connect:SendSSHPublicKey. For more information, see&nbsp;Uninstall EC2 Instance Connect&nbsp;and&nbsp;Configure IAM Permissions for EC2 Instance Connect.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Add a rule for inbound&nbsp;SSH&nbsp;traffic to a&nbsp;Linux&nbsp;instance<br />\r\n&nbsp;<br />\r\nSecurity groups act as a firewall for associated instances, controlling both inbound and outbound traffic at the instance level. You must add rules to a security group to enable you to connect to your&nbsp;Linux&nbsp;instance from your IP address using&nbsp;SSH.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>To add a rule to a security group for inbound&nbsp;SSH&nbsp;traffic over IPv4 (console)<br />\r\nOpen the Amazon EC2 console at&nbsp;https://console.aws.amazon.com/ec2/.<br />\r\nFrom the top navigation bar, select a Region for the security group. Security groups are specific to a Region, so you should select the same Region in which you created your instance.<br />\r\nIn the navigation pane, choose&nbsp;Instances.<br />\r\nSelect your instance and, in bottom half of the screen, choose the&nbsp;Security&nbsp;tab.&nbsp;Security groups&nbsp;lists the security groups that are associated with the instance.&nbsp;Inbound rules&nbsp;displays a list of the inbound rules that are in effect for the instance.<br />\r\nFor the security group to which you&#39;ll add the new rule, choose the security group ID link to open the security group.<br />\r\nOn the&nbsp;Inbound rules&nbsp;tab, choose&nbsp;Edit inbound rules.<br />\r\nOn the&nbsp;Edit inbound rules&nbsp;page, do the following:</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Choose&nbsp;Add rule.<br />\r\nFor&nbsp;Type, choose&nbsp;SSH.<br />\r\nFor&nbsp;Source, choose&nbsp;My IP&nbsp;to automatically populate the field with the public IPv4 address of your local computer.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Alternatively, for&nbsp;Source, choose&nbsp;Custom&nbsp;and enter the public IPv4 address of your computer or network in CIDR notation. For example, if your IPv4 address is&nbsp;203.0.113.25, enter&nbsp;203.0.113.25/32&nbsp;to list this single IPv4 address in CIDR notation. If your company allocates addresses from a range, enter the entire range, such as&nbsp;203.0.113.0/24.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>For information about finding your IP address, see&nbsp;Before you start.<br />\r\nChoose&nbsp;Save rules.<br />\r\nIf you launched an instance with an IPv6 address and want to connect to your instance using its IPv6 address, you must add rules that allow inbound IPv6 traffic over&nbsp;SSH.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>To add a rule to a security group for inbound&nbsp;SSH&nbsp;traffic over IPv6 (console)<br />\r\nOpen the Amazon EC2 console at&nbsp;https://console.aws.amazon.com/ec2/.<br />\r\nFrom the top navigation bar, select a Region for the security group. Security groups are specific to a Region, so you should select the same Region in which you created your instance.<br />\r\nIn the navigation pane, choose&nbsp;Instances.<br />\r\nSelect your instance and, in bottom half of the screen, choose the&nbsp;Security&nbsp;tab.&nbsp;Security groups&nbsp;lists the security groups that are associated with the instance.&nbsp;Inbound rules&nbsp;displays a list of the inbound rules that are in effect for the instance.<br />\r\nFor the security group to which you&#39;ll add the new rule, choose the security group ID link to open the security group.<br />\r\nOn the&nbsp;Inbound rules&nbsp;tab, choose&nbsp;Edit inbound rules.<br />\r\nOn the&nbsp;Edit inbound rules&nbsp;page, do the following:</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Choose&nbsp;Add rule.<br />\r\nFor&nbsp;Type, choose&nbsp;SSH.<br />\r\nFor&nbsp;Source, choose&nbsp;Custom&nbsp;and enter the IPv6 address of your computer in CIDR notation. For example, if your IPv6 address is&nbsp;2001:db8:1234:1a00:9691:9503:25ad:1761, enter&nbsp;2001:db8:1234:1a00:9691:9503:25ad:1761/128&nbsp;to list the single IP address in CIDR notation. If your company allocates addresses from a range, enter the entire range, such as&nbsp;2001:db8:1234:1a00::/64.<br />\r\nChoose&nbsp;Save rules.<br />\r\nNote<br />\r\nBe sure to run the following commands on your local system, not on the instance itself. For more information about these command line interfaces, see&nbsp;Access Amazon EC2.<br />\r\nTo add a rule to a security group using the command line<br />\r\nFind the security group that is associated with your instance using one of the following commands:</p>\r\n\r\n<p>describe-instance-attribute&nbsp;(AWS CLI)</p>\r\n\r\n<p><code>&nbsp;</code><br />\r\n<code>aws ec2 describe-instance-attribute --region region --instance-id instance_id --attribute groupSet</code><br />\r\n<code>Get-EC2InstanceAttribute&nbsp;(AWS Tools for Windows PowerShell)</code></p>\r\n\r\n<p><code>&nbsp;</code><br />\r\n<code>PS C:&gt; (Get-EC2InstanceAttribute -Region region -InstanceId instance_id -Attribute groupSet).Groups</code></p>\r\n\r\n<p><br />\r\nBoth commands return a security group ID, which you use in the next step.<br />\r\nAdd the rule to the security group using one of the following commands:</p>\r\n\r\n<p>authorize-security-group-ingress&nbsp;(AWS CLI)</p>\r\n\r\n<p>&nbsp;<br />\r\n<code>aws ec2 authorize-security-group-ingress --region region --group-id security_group_id --protocol tcp --port 22 --cidr cidr_ip_range</code><br />\r\n<code>Grant-EC2SecurityGroupIngress&nbsp;(AWS Tools for Windows PowerShell)</code></p>\r\n\r\n<p>The&nbsp;Grant-EC2SecurityGroupIngress&nbsp;command needs an&nbsp;IpPermission&nbsp;parameter, which describes the protocol, port range, and IP address range to be used for the security group rule. The following command creates the&nbsp;IpPermission&nbsp;parameter:</p>\r\n\r\n<p><code>&nbsp;</code><br />\r\n<code>PS C:&gt; $ip1 = @{ IpProtocol=&quot;tcp&quot;; FromPort=&quot;22&quot;; ToPort=&quot;22&quot;; IpRanges=&quot;cidr_ip_range&quot; }</code><br />\r\n<code>&nbsp;</code><br />\r\n<code>PS C:&gt; Grant-EC2SecurityGroupIngress -Region region -GroupId security_group_id -IpPermission @($ip1)</code></p>\r\n\r\n<p><br />\r\nAssign a security group to an instance<br />\r\n&nbsp;<br />\r\nYou can assign a security group to an instance when you launch the instance. When you add or remove rules, those changes are automatically applied to all instances to which you&#39;ve assigned the security group.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\r\n',	NULL,	NULL,	NULL),
(33,	1,	25,	1,	'Check the modulus on a certificate',	'',	'<p>openssl req -noout -modulus -in $csr</p>\r\n',	NULL,	NULL,	NULL),
(34,	1,	25,	1,	'Parse file names with spaces',	'',	'<pre>\r\n<samp><code>#!/bin/bash</code>\r\n\r\n<code>OIFS=&quot;$IFS&quot;</code>\r\n<code>IFS=$&#39;\n&#39;</code>\r\n\r\n<code>if [[ $# == 0 ]]; then</code>\r\n<code>echo &quot;ERROR: This script requires at least 1 argument&quot; </code>\r\n<code>exit</code>\r\n<code>fi</code>\r\n\r\n<code>for line in `cat ./$1MailAttachments.txt`; do cp &quot;$line&quot; /data2/Apps/tmp/leo/$1/MailAttachments/; done</code>\r\n\r\n<code>IFS=&quot;$OIFS&quot;</code></samp></pre>\r\n',	NULL,	NULL,	NULL),
(35,	1,	1,	1,	'Generate new ca certificate (BSD)',	'',	'<p><code>$ openssh genrsa -aes256 cacert.key 4096</code></p>\r\n\r\n<p><code>$ openssl req -x509 -new -nodes -key cacert.key -sha256 -days 365 -out cacert.pem</code></p>\r\n\r\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Now you can use cacert.pem as the certificate authority for generating certs for vpn, ldap, WiFi and such</p>\r\n',	NULL,	NULL,	NULL),
(36,	1,	25,	1,	'Remove all .svn folders',	'',	'<p><code>find -name &quot;.svn&quot; | xargs /bin/rm -rf</code></p>\r\n',	NULL,	NULL,	NULL),
(37,	1,	25,	1,	'Query to return size in MB of a database',	'',	'<p><tt><code>SELECT TABLE_SCHEMA AS `Database`,</code></tt></p>\r\n\r\n<p><tt><code>TABLE_NAME AS `Table`,</code></tt></p>\r\n\r\n<p><tt><code>ROUND((DATA_LENGTH + INDEX_LENGTH) / 1024 / 1024) AS `Size (MB)`</code></tt></p>\r\n\r\n<p><tt><code>FROM information_schema.TABLES WHERE table_schema = &#39;Some Database&#39;</code></tt></p>\r\n',	NULL,	NULL,	NULL),
(44,	1,	25,	1,	'Python/Pip - Fix for error: externally-managed-environment',	'',	'<p>A command like this gives the externally managed error</p>\r\n\r\n<p><code>pip install -r requirements.txt</code></p>\r\n\r\n<p>Long story: Your distribution is trying to protect you against mixing&nbsp;apt&nbsp;provided packages and&nbsp;pip&nbsp;provided packages. Mixing two package managers (apt&nbsp;and&nbsp;pip&nbsp;here) is always a bad idea and the source of many issues.<br />\r\n<br />\r\nMost likely python3 is installed by your OS package manager but you are trying to install dependencies with pip3 so you need to create a new virtual environment and install python3 in it to use</p>\r\n\r\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\r\n\r\n<p><code>python3 -m venv /tmp/venv</code></p>\r\n\r\n<p><code>source /tmp/venv/bin/activate</code></p>\r\n\r\n<p><code>pip3 install -r requirements.txt</code></p>\r\n',	NULL,	NULL,	NULL),
(50,	1,	1,	1,	'Monitor HAProxy with Zabbix',	'',	'<p>I wanted to monitor the haproxy metrics and read some examples from others using special scripts and also using unix socket and socat. I made some progress but wanted a simpler solution that would be more flexible and easy to add more items when new services were added to haproxy&#39;s config.<br />\r\n<br />\r\nHere is my solution:<br />\r\n<br />\r\nI first need to allow accessing the stats via http. So in the haproxy.cfg file I added:<br />\r\n<br />\r\nlisten admin<br />\r\nbind *:8080<br />\r\nstats enable<br />\r\nstats auth admin:&lt;MyPASSWORD&gt;<br />\r\n<br />\r\nI use curl to access the metrics in csv format. So on the haproxy host I made sure curl was installed (it already was) and then added the following zabbiz config file:<br />\r\n<br />\r\n/etc/zabbix/zabbix_agentd.d/userparameter_haproxy.conf<br />\r\n<br />\r\nIn it I placed:<br />\r\n<br />\r\nUserParameter=haproxy.stats[*], curl -u admin:&lt;MyPASSWORD&gt; &quot;http://localhost:8080/haproxy?stats;csv&quot; 2&gt;/dev/null | grep &quot;^$1,$2&quot; | cut -d, -f $3<br />\r\n<br />\r\nNow its simple, all I need to do is send 3 params: proxyname, servicename and the column number (+1) of the metric I&#39;m looking for.<br />\r\n<br />\r\nSo from the zabbix server I can do:<br />\r\n<br />\r\n&gt; zabbix_get -s haproxy_hostname -k [haproxy.stats[&quot;ProxyName&quot;,&quot;BACKEND&quot;,&quot;18&quot;]<br />\r\n&lt; OK<br />\r\n<br />\r\nNOTE: passing column # 18 is actualy metric item 17 as reported in the CSV table here:&nbsp;<a href=\"http://cbonte.github.io/haproxy-dconv/configuration-1.4.html#9.1\" target=\"_blank\">http://cbonte.github.io/haproxy-dcon...n-1.4.html#9.1</a><br />\r\n<br />\r\nNow its easy to add zabbix items for all the metrics needed from haproxy.</p>\r\n',	NULL,	NULL,	NULL),
(51,	1,	2,	1,	'Spam reduction with spamassassin - postfix/dovecot',	'',	'<p>$ apt install spamassassin spamc</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Find the following line in /etc/postfix/master.cf</p>\r\n\r\n<p>smtp &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;inet &nbsp;n &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; - &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; y &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; - &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; - &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; smtpd</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Change it to&nbsp;</p>\r\n\r\n<p>smtp &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;inet &nbsp;n &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; - &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; y &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; - &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; - &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; smtpd -o content_filter=spamassassin</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Add this to the bottom of the file</p>\r\n\r\n<p>spamassassin unix - &nbsp; &nbsp; n &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; n &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; - &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; - &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; pipe<br />\r\n&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; user=spamd argv=/usr/bin/spamc -f -e<br />\r\n&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; /usr/sbin/sendmail -oi -f ${sender} ${recipient}<br />\r\n&nbsp;</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Save the file and edit /etc/spamassassin/local.cf</p>\r\n\r\n<p>It should look something like this</p>\r\n\r\n<blockquote>\r\n<p><code># report_contact youremailaddress@domain.tld<br />\r\n# &nbsp; Add *****SPAM***** to the Subject header of spam e-mails<br />\r\n&nbsp;rewrite_header Subject **Warning: Potential SPAM<br />\r\nreport_safe 0<br />\r\n# trusted_networks 212.17.35.<br />\r\n# lock_method flock<br />\r\nrequired_score 4.0<br />\r\n# &nbsp; Use Bayesian classifier (default: 1)<br />\r\n# use_bayes 1<br />\r\n# &nbsp; Bayesian classifier auto-learning (default: 1)<br />\r\n# bayes_auto_learn 1<br />\r\nuse_bayes_rules 1<br />\r\n# bayes_ignore_header X-Bogosity<br />\r\n# bayes_ignore_header X-Spam-Flag<br />\r\n# bayes_ignore_header X-Spam-Status<br />\r\n# normalize_charset 1<br />\r\n# &nbsp; Textual body scan limit &nbsp; &nbsp;(default: 50000)<br />\r\n# body_part_scan_size 50000<br />\r\n# &nbsp; Textual rawbody data scan limit &nbsp; &nbsp;(default: 500000)<br />\r\n# rawbody_part_scan_size 500000<br />\r\n# &nbsp; Some shortcircuiting, if the plugin is enabled</code></p>\r\n\r\n<p><code>ifplugin Mail::SpamAssassin::Plugin::Shortcircuit<br />\r\n# shortcircuit USER_IN_WELCOMELIST &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; on<br />\r\n# shortcircuit USER_IN_DEF_WELCOMELIST &nbsp; on<br />\r\n# shortcircuit USER_IN_ALL_SPAM_TO &nbsp; &nbsp; on<br />\r\n# shortcircuit USER_IN_BLOCKLIST &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; on<br />\r\n# shortcircuit USER_IN_BLOCKLIST_TO &nbsp; &nbsp;on<br />\r\n# shortcircuit ALL_TRUSTED &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; on<br />\r\n# &nbsp; and a well-trained bayes DB can save running rules, too<br />\r\n# shortcircuit BAYES_99 &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;spam<br />\r\n# shortcircuit BAYES_00 &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;ham<br />\r\nendif # Mail::SpamAssassin::Plugin::Shortcircuit<br />\r\nrewrite_header subject ** WARNING: Potential SPAM SCORE: _SCORE_<br />\r\nuse_bayes &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; 1<br />\r\nuse_bayes_rules &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; 1<br />\r\nbayes_auto_learn &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;1<br />\r\nskip_rbl_checks &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; 0<br />\r\nuse_razor2 &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;0<br />\r\nuse_pyzor &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; 0# Rewrite the subject of suspected spam e-mails<br />\r\n# Just add an X-Spam-Report header to suspected spam, rather than rewriting the content of the e-mail<br />\r\nreport_safe 0<br />\r\n# Also we want to add a detailed ham report header to even e-mail that ISN&#39;T suspected to be spam<br />\r\nadd_header spam SPAM-Report _REPORT_<br />\r\n# Set the threshold at which a message is considered spam (3 is usually sufficient)<br />\r\nrequired_score 1.0<br />\r\nheader Viagra Subject =~ /Viagra/<br />\r\nscore Viagra 5<br />\r\nheader Wijziging Subject =~ /Wijziging in uw energietarief/<br />\r\nscore Wijziging 4<br />\r\nheader Earn Subject =~ /Earn up to/<br />\r\nscore Earn 4</code></p>\r\n</blockquote>\r\n\r\n<p><code>$systemcrestart spamd</code></p>\r\n\r\n<p><code>$systemctl restart postfix</code></p>\r\n\r\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\r\n\r\n<p><code><img alt=\"\" src=\"/perl/images/spam.png\" style=\"height:152px; width:800px\" /></code></p>\r\n\r\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\r\n\r\n<p>JOB DONE</p>\r\n\r\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\r\n\r\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\r\n',	NULL,	NULL,	NULL),
(52,	1,	2,	1,	'Setup A Redis Cluster',	'<h2><strong>What Is Redis?</strong></h2>\r\n\r\n<p>Redis cluster is mostly used by applications with high loads or by applications that need their data to be stored across several servers. Or by applications with a lot of traffic.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>For example: Suppose that you have a single server for Redis. The server gets overloaded with tons of requests at a given time. Since Redis is single-threaded it could only answer a single request at a time. If you need your Redis server to handle more than one request at a time, then you need to set up a Redis cluster.</p>\r\n\r\n<h2><strong>Benefits of Redis Cluster</strong></h2>\r\n\r\n<ul>\r\n	<li>The ability to scale your Redis server</li>\r\n	<li>The ability to run Redis across several nodes</li>\r\n	<li>The ability to minimize failover and maximize availability</li>\r\n	<li>The ability to handle more load</li>\r\n</ul>\r\n',	'<h2>There are two main ways to begin initial setup</h2>\r\n\r\n<ul>\r\n	<li>On a production environment with several nodes (server)</li>\r\n	<li>On a local machine as a test</li>\r\n</ul>\r\n\r\n<p>Setting up a Redis cluster is to cluster several nodes (servers) together as a Redis cluster. This Redis cluster setup is of a production environment.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Before starting to set up this method we need at least 6 servers so I begin with a single VM&nbsp;</p>\r\n\r\n<h2>SSH into your Remote Server</h2>\r\n\r\n<p>The first step is to SSH into your remote server by typing the command below:</p>\r\n\r\n<pre>\r\n<code>ssh [USERNAME]@[IP_ADDRESS]</code></pre>\r\n\r\n<p><img alt=\"\" src=\"/perl/images/ssh.png\" /></p>\r\n\r\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\r\n\r\n<h3><strong>Update your Linux packages</strong></h3>\r\n\r\n<p>After getting into your Linux server make sure you update its packages. To update your Linux packages, type the command below:</p>\r\n\r\n<div style=\"background:#eeeeee; border:1px solid #cccccc; padding:5px 10px\"><code>$ sudo apt update&nbsp;</code></div>\r\n\r\n<h2><strong>Output:</strong></h2>\r\n\r\n<div style=\"background:#eeeeee; border:1px solid #cccccc; padding:5px 10px\"><code>Get:31 http://deb.debian.org/debian bookworm-backports/non-free-firmware Translation-en [2.848 B]<br />\r\nGet:32 http://deb.debian.org/debian bookworm-backports/main Sources [278 kB]<br />\r\nGet:33 http://deb.debian.org/debian bookworm-backports/main amd64 Packages [247 kB]<br />\r\nGet:34 http://deb.debian.org/debian bookworm-backports/main Translation-en [207 kB]<br />\r\nFetched 1.658 kB in 6s (276 kB/s) &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;&nbsp;<br />\r\nReading package lists... Done<br />\r\nBuilding dependency tree... Done<br />\r\nReading state information... Done</code><br />\r\n&nbsp;</div>\r\n\r\n<h3>Install Redis</h3>\r\n\r\n<p>A Redis cluster node requires to have Redis installed. To install Redis on your server type the command below:</p>\r\n\r\n<div style=\"background:#eeeeee; border:1px solid #cccccc; padding:5px 10px\"><code>$ sudo apt install redis-server</code></div>\r\n\r\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\r\n\r\n<h2>Output:</h2>\r\n\r\n<div style=\"background:#eeeeee; border:1px solid #cccccc; padding:5px 10px\">peter@jenkins:~$ sudo apt install redis-server<br />\r\nReading package lists... Done<br />\r\nBuilding dependency tree... Done<br />\r\nReading state information... Done<br />\r\nThe following additional packages will be installed:<br />\r\n&nbsp; liblzf1 redis-tools<br />\r\nSuggested packages:<br />\r\n&nbsp; ruby-redis<br />\r\nThe following NEW packages will be installed:<br />\r\n&nbsp; liblzf1 redis-server redis-tools<br />\r\n0 upgraded, 3 newly installed, 0 to remove and 284 not upgraded.<br />\r\nNeed to get 1.072 kB of archives.<br />\r\nAfter this operation, 6.202 kB of additional disk space will be used.<br />\r\nDo you want to continue? [Y/n] y<br />\r\nGet:1 http://deb.debian.org/debian bookworm/main amd64 liblzf1 amd64 3.6-3 [10,2 kB]<br />\r\nGet:2 http://deb.debian.org/debian bookworm/main amd64 redis-tools amd64 5:7.0.15-1~deb12u1 [989 kB]<br />\r\nGet:3 http://deb.debian.org/debian bookworm/main amd64 redis-server amd64 5:7.0.15-1~deb12u1 [72,4 kB]<br />\r\nFetched 1.072 kB in 0s (3.690 kB/s) &nbsp; &nbsp;<br />\r\nSelecting previously unselected package liblzf1:amd64.<br />\r\n(Reading database ... 280106 files and directories currently installed.)<br />\r\nPreparing to unpack .../liblzf1_3.6-3_amd64.deb ...<br />\r\nUnpacking liblzf1:amd64 (3.6-3) ...<br />\r\nSelecting previously unselected package redis-tools.<br />\r\nPreparing to unpack .../redis-tools_5%3a7.0.15-1~deb12u1_amd64.deb ...<br />\r\nUnpacking redis-tools (5:7.0.15-1~deb12u1) ...<br />\r\nSelecting previously unselected package redis-server.<br />\r\nPreparing to unpack .../redis-server_5%3a7.0.15-1~deb12u1_amd64.deb ...<br />\r\nUnpacking redis-server (5:7.0.15-1~deb12u1) ...<br />\r\nSetting up liblzf1:amd64 (3.6-3) ...<br />\r\nSetting up redis-tools (5:7.0.15-1~deb12u1) ...<br />\r\nSetting up redis-server (5:7.0.15-1~deb12u1) ...<br />\r\nCreated symlink /etc/systemd/system/redis.service &rarr; /lib/systemd/system/redis-server.service.<br />\r\nCreated symlink /etc/systemd/system/multi-user.target.wants/redis-server.service &rarr; /lib/syste<br />\r\nmd/system/redis-server.service.<br />\r\nProcessing triggers for man-db (2.11.2-2) ...<br />\r\nProcessing triggers for libc-bin (2.36-9+deb12u7) ...<br />\r\npeter@jenkins:~$&nbsp;<br />\r\n&nbsp;</div>\r\n\r\n<h3>Enable Redis</h3>\r\n\r\n<p>After successfully installing Redis on a node, start and then enable it at every boot. So that your Redis server would start after you restart your server.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>To start and enable the Redis server type the command below:</p>\r\n\r\n<div style=\"background:#eeeeee; border:1px solid #cccccc; padding:5px 10px\"><code>systemctl start redis<br />\r\nsystemctl enable redis-server</code></div>\r\n\r\n<p>Additionally, you could also check the status of the Redis server by typing the command below:</p>\r\n\r\n<div style=\"background:#eeeeee; border:1px solid #cccccc; padding:5px 10px\"><code>systemctl status redis</code></div>\r\n\r\n<h2>Output:</h2>\r\n\r\n<div style=\"background:#eeeeee; border:1px solid #cccccc; padding:5px 10px\">&acirc;&mdash;&Acirc;&Acirc;&Acirc;&Acirc;&Acirc;&Acirc;&Acirc;&Acirc;&Acirc; redis-server.service - Advanced key-value store<br />\r\n&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Loaded: loaded (/lib/systemd/system/redis-server.service; enabled; preset: enabled)<br />\r\n&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Active: active (running) since Tue 2024-10-01 11:43:58 CEST; 3min 56s ago<br />\r\n&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Docs: http://redis.io/documentation,<br />\r\n&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;man:redis-server(1)<br />\r\n&nbsp; &nbsp;Main PID: 143528 (redis-server)<br />\r\n&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Status: &quot;Ready to accept connections&quot;<br />\r\n&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; Tasks: 5 (limit: 4592)<br />\r\n&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Memory: 8.7M<br />\r\n&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; CPU: 455ms<br />\r\n&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;CGroup: /system.slice/redis-server.service<br />\r\n&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;&acirc;&rdquo;&rdquo;&acirc;&rdquo;&euro;143528 &quot;/usr/bin/redis-server 127.0.0.1:6379&quot;<br />\r\n&nbsp;</div>\r\n\r\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\r\n\r\n<h3>Configure redis.conf File</h3>\r\n\r\n<div style=\"background:#eeeeee; border:1px solid #cccccc; padding:5px 10px\"><code>sudo vi /etc/redis/redis.conf</code></div>\r\n\r\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\r\n\r\n<p>After opening up the configuration file, you need to change a few parameters. The file contains a lot of text, so you need to find the parameter you need by hovering. Also, be sure to change this file on each server individually.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>The first parameter to be changed is&nbsp;<strong>bind.&nbsp;</strong>This parameter defines which interfaces should Redis listen to. By default, it listens for all connections. But we could alter it to listen to one or more interfaces.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Find the word&nbsp;<strong>bind&nbsp;</strong>and add your server&rsquo;s private IP address after&nbsp;<strong>127.0.0.1</strong>&nbsp;as shown&nbsp; below.</p>\r\n\r\n<div style=\"background:#eeeeee; border:1px solid #cccccc; padding:5px 10px\"><code>bind 127.0.0.1 [SERVER_IP_ADDRESS]</code></div>\r\n\r\n<p><img alt=\"\" src=\"/perl/images/ssh2.png\" /></p>\r\n\r\n<p>Add your private IP address</p>\r\n\r\n<p>The second parameter is&nbsp;<strong>protected-mode</strong>. This parameter simply protects your Redis server from outside connections and hosts. By default, it is on. But for making a Redis cluster we need to disable it by changing its values to&nbsp;<strong>no</strong>.</p>\r\n\r\n<div style=\"background:#eeeeee; border:1px solid #cccccc; padding:5px 10px\"><code>protected-mode no</code></div>\r\n\r\n<p>The next parameter is&nbsp;<strong>appendonly</strong>. This parameter is an alternative persistence mode that provides much better durability. It ensures data protection even if your server running Redis accidentally stops or the power goes off.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>By enabling this option you trade off some speed in return for better data protection.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>To enable&nbsp;<strong>appendonly</strong>&nbsp;mode, change its values to&nbsp;<strong>yes</strong>.</p>\r\n\r\n<div style=\"background:#eeeeee; border:1px solid #cccccc; padding:5px 10px\"><code>appendonly yes</code></div>\r\n\r\n<p>The last parameters are related to clustering configurations, which you need to&nbsp;<strong>uncomment</strong>&nbsp;in the file.</p>\r\n\r\n<ul>\r\n	<li><strong>cluster-enabled:&nbsp;</strong>This parameter enables the cluster mode and disables the default standalone mode.</li>\r\n	<li><strong>cluster-config-file:&nbsp;</strong>This parameter specifies the file name where the cluster configurations live.</li>\r\n	<li><strong>cluster-node-timeout:&nbsp;</strong>This parameter specifies the amount of time (in milliseconds) in which a node would be considered failed. You could also change its values if you&rsquo;d like.</li>\r\n</ul>\r\n\r\n<div style=\"background:#eeeeee; border:1px solid #cccccc; padding:5px 10px\"><code>cluster-enabled yes<br />\r\ncluster-config-file nodes-6379.conf<br />\r\ncluster-node-timeout 15000</code></div>\r\n\r\n<p>After successfully editing all those lines you could quit the editor and save your changes.</p>\r\n\r\n<h3>Restart Redis Server</h3>\r\n\r\n<p>Now that the Redis configuration file is ready we need to restart the Redis server to implement the changes.</p>\r\n\r\n<div style=\"background:#eeeeee; border:1px solid #cccccc; padding:5px 10px\"><code>systemctl restart redis</code></div>\r\n\r\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\r\n\r\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\r\n\r\n<p>To check the Redis server for any changes you cail the logs and you should see a node ID</p>\r\n\r\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\r\n\r\n<h3>Open Ports&nbsp;<strong>6397</strong>&nbsp;and&nbsp;<strong>16379</strong></h3>\r\n\r\n<p>Since each one of the Redis nodes would be talking via ports 6397 and 16379, it is necessary to open these ports for outside connection by typing the commands below:</p>\r\n\r\n<h2><code>IPTables:</code></h2>\r\n\r\n<div style=\"background:#eeeeee; border:1px solid #cccccc; padding:5px 10px\"><code>sudo iptables -A INPUT -p tcp --dport 6379 -j ACCEPT<br />\r\nsudo iptables -A INPUT -p tcp --dport 16379 -j ACCEPT</code></div>\r\n\r\n<h2>UFW:</h2>\r\n\r\n<div style=\"background:#eeeeee; border:1px solid #cccccc; padding:5px 10px\">sudo ufw allow 6379<br />\r\nsudo ufw allow 16379</div>\r\n\r\n<h3>Create the Redis Cluster</h3>\r\n\r\n<p>The last step toward creating a Redis cluster is to use the redis-cli tool on a Redis node for creating a cluster. This command required all the private IP addresses of your nodes.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>This command requires an even amount of IP addresses. The first few would be considered master nodes, and the rest would be considered replica nodes.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>First I create five clones of my VM and put them on different ip addresses so I cave 3 master nodes and 3 replication nodes on the ip range 192.168.2.11-16 then log into the first masternode</p>\r\n\r\n<p>For example: If you have defined 6 IP addresses, then after running this command the first 3 would be considered master nodes and the remaining 3 would be replica nodes.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>We install the redis-cli util with the following commands</p>\r\n\r\n<div style=\"background:#eeeeee; border:1px solid #cccccc; padding:5px 10px\"><code>sudo apt-get install lsb-release curl gpg<br />\r\ncurl -fsSL https://packages.redis.io/gpg | sudo gpg --dearmor -o /usr/share/keyrings/redis-archive-keyring.gpg<br />\r\nsudo chmod 644 /usr/share/keyrings/redis-archive-keyring.gpg<br />\r\necho &quot;deb [signed-by=/usr/share/keyrings/redis-archive-keyring.gpg] https://packages.redis.io/deb $(lsb_release -cs) main&quot; | sudo tee /etc/apt/sources.list.d/redis.list<br />\r\nsudo apt-get update<br />\r\nsudo apt-get install redis</code></div>\r\n\r\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\r\n\r\n<p><strong>Note!</strong>&nbsp;You should only run this command on a single node.</p>\r\n\r\n<div style=\"background:#eeeeee; border:1px solid #cccccc; padding:5px 10px\"><code>redis-cli --cluster create 192.168.2.11:6379 192.168.2.12:6379 192.168.2.13:6379 192.168.2.14:6379 192.168.2.15:6379 192.168.2.16:6379 --cluster-replicas 1</code></div>\r\n\r\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\r\n\r\n<h3>Et voil&agrave;</h3>\r\n\r\n<div style=\"background:#eeeeee; border:1px solid #cccccc; padding:5px 10px\">&gt;&gt;&gt; Performing hash slots allocation on 6 nodes...<br />\r\nMaster[0] -&gt; Slots 0 - 5460<br />\r\nMaster[1] -&gt; Slots 5461 - 10922<br />\r\nMaster[2] -&gt; Slots 10923 - 16383<br />\r\nAdding replica 192.168.2.15:6379 to 192.168.2.11:6379<br />\r\nAdding replica 192.168.2.16:6379 to 192.168.2.12:6379<br />\r\nAdding replica 192.168.2.14:6379 to 192.168.2.13:6379<br />\r\nM: 78856dad1e1905da0c0683ecf764be6b4e0d7820 192.168.2.11:6379<br />\r\n&nbsp; &nbsp;slots:[0-5460] (5461 slots) master<br />\r\nM: f9bf3fe34a2f1e66cd403829948b1e1cd6dd3357 192.168.2.12:6379<br />\r\n&nbsp; &nbsp;slots:[5461-10922] (5462 slots) master<br />\r\nM: 38872371b9257929bf6b9516d130659458bb6086 192.168.2.13:6379<br />\r\n&nbsp; &nbsp;slots:[10923-16383] (5461 slots) master<br />\r\nS: 1252965a14101a9c484f8eaf151b348bafe26d05 192.168.2.14:6379<br />\r\n&nbsp; &nbsp;replicates 38872371b9257929bf6b9516d130659458bb6086<br />\r\nS: 008ae81691c905c49a410a1006ec1aa678c2a9e7 192.168.2.15:6379<br />\r\n&nbsp; &nbsp;replicates 78856dad1e1905da0c0683ecf764be6b4e0d7820<br />\r\nS: fe6a1d7de8d3114892aff7cfbf88d899da4b6777 192.168.2.16:6379<br />\r\n&nbsp; &nbsp;replicates f9bf3fe34a2f1e66cd403829948b1e1cd6dd3357<br />\r\nCan I set the above configuration? (type &#39;yes&#39; to accept): yes<br />\r\n&gt;&gt;&gt; Nodes configuration updated<br />\r\n&gt;&gt;&gt; Assign a different config epoch to each node<br />\r\n&gt;&gt;&gt; Sending CLUSTER MEET messages to join the cluster<br />\r\nWaiting for the cluster to join<br />\r\n.<br />\r\n&gt;&gt;&gt; Performing Cluster Check (using node 192.168.2.11:6379)<br />\r\nM: 78856dad1e1905da0c0683ecf764be6b4e0d7820 192.168.2.11:6379<br />\r\n&nbsp; &nbsp;slots:[0-5460] (5461 slots) master<br />\r\n&nbsp; &nbsp;1 additional replica(s)<br />\r\nS: 008ae81691c905c49a410a1006ec1aa678c2a9e7 192.168.2.15:6379<br />\r\n&nbsp; &nbsp;slots: (0 slots) slave<br />\r\n&nbsp; &nbsp;replicates 78856dad1e1905da0c0683ecf764be6b4e0d7820<br />\r\nM: f9bf3fe34a2f1e66cd403829948b1e1cd6dd3357 192.168.2.12:6379<br />\r\n&nbsp; &nbsp;slots:[5461-10922] (5462 slots) master<br />\r\n&nbsp; &nbsp;1 additional replica(s)<br />\r\nM: 38872371b9257929bf6b9516d130659458bb6086 192.168.2.13:6379<br />\r\n&nbsp; &nbsp;slots:[10923-16383] (5461 slots) master<br />\r\n&nbsp; &nbsp;1 additional replica(s)<br />\r\nS: 1252965a14101a9c484f8eaf151b348bafe26d05 192.168.2.14:6379<br />\r\n&nbsp; &nbsp;slots: (0 slots) slave<br />\r\n&nbsp; &nbsp;replicates 38872371b9257929bf6b9516d130659458bb6086<br />\r\nS: fe6a1d7de8d3114892aff7cfbf88d899da4b6777 192.168.2.16:6379<br />\r\n&nbsp; &nbsp;slots: (0 slots) slave<br />\r\n&nbsp; &nbsp;replicates f9bf3fe34a2f1e66cd403829948b1e1cd6dd3357<br />\r\n[OK] All nodes agree about slots configuration.<br />\r\n&gt;&gt;&gt; Check for open slots...<br />\r\n&gt;&gt;&gt; Check slots coverage...<br />\r\n[OK] All 16384 slots covered.</div>\r\n\r\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\r\n\r\n<h3>Monitoring with Zabbix</h3>\r\n\r\n<p>If you have access to a Zabbix server you can install zabbix-agent2 on each node and set up a new host for each node using the Ridis By ZabbixAgent2 template</p>\r\n\r\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\r\n\r\n<p><img width=100% alt=\"\" src=\"/perl/images/redis.png\" /></p>\r\n',	NULL,	NULL,	NULL),
(54,	1,	2,	1,	'Setup Redis Sentinel cluster in Ubuntu',	'<p>Here on Public IP Address 34.0.0.1 we have our Redis Master Server running and also Redis Sentinel running. On IPs 34.0.0.2 and 34.0.0.3 we have Redis Slave and Redis Sentinel running.</p>\r\n\r\n<p><strong>Installation Steps</strong></p>\r\n\r\n<p>Install Redis Server and Redis Sentinel on all 3 Nodes</p>\r\n\r\n<ul>\r\n	<li><strong>sudo add-apt-repository ppa:redislabs/redis</strong></li>\r\n	<li><strong>sudo apt-get -y update</strong></li>\r\n	<li><strong>sudo apt-get -y install redis redis-sentinel net-tools</strong></li>\r\n</ul>\r\n',	'<p><strong>This Guide is just a quickie.</strong></p>\r\n\r\n<p><strong>What do you need?</strong></p>\r\n\r\n<p>4 VMs, 1 for Hapoxy (software Load Balancer), 3 for the redis/sentinel nodes. Each redis node also runs redis-sentinel although it is possible to have the sentinels on their own IPs but that would double the number of servers required.<br />\r\nOS: I have chosen Ubuntu 24.04 LTS Server edition (you could use any Linux flavor)<br />\r\nHardware: 4GB RAM, 1 cpu cores, 12GB hard disk</p>\r\n\r\n<p><strong>Network:</strong><br />\r\nHAProxy: 192.168.2.40<br />\r\nNodes: 192.168.2.51-53</p>\r\n\r\n<p><img alt=\"\" src=\"/perl/images/RedisTopology.png\" style=\"width:80%\" /></p>\r\n\r\n<p><strong>Installation Steps</strong></p>\r\n\r\n<p>I used KVM on a bridged network, virsh,virsh-sysprep to create the VMS, but you can do it with whatever yuo want so long as you end up with 4 VMS</p>\r\n\r\n<p><img alt=\"\" src=\"/perl/images/kvm.png\" style=\"height:367px; width:474px\" /></p>\r\n\r\n<p><strong>Redis</strong></p>\r\n\r\n<p>Install Redis Server and Redis Sentinel on all 3 Nodes</p>\r\n\r\n<ul>\r\n	<li><strong>sudo add-apt-repository ppa:redislabs/redis</strong></li>\r\n	<li><strong>sudo apt-get -y update</strong></li>\r\n	<li><strong>sudo apt-get -y install redis redis-sentinel net-tools</strong></li>\r\n</ul>\r\n\r\n<p>Now after installing you will be able to see redis-server running on port 6379 and redis-sentinel running on port 26379.<br />\r\nCommand &mdash;<strong> ps -eaf | grep redis</strong></p>\r\n\r\n<p><strong>Configure Redis Master and Slave</strong></p>\r\n\r\n<p>Lets configure the <strong>Master Node 192.168.2.51 on /etc/redis/redis.conf&nbsp;</strong> // NOTE: I&#39;m using requirepass here but perhaps it creates more problems than it solves (especially for clients)<br />\r\n<code>bind 0.0.0.0 (or you can comment it for redis client to connect)<br />\r\nprotected-mode no<br />\r\nport 6379<br />\r\ndaemonized yes<br />\r\nsupervised systemd<br />\r\nmasterauth ******<br />\r\nrequirepass ******</code></p>\r\n\r\n<p>Do not change other values unless its necessary.</p>\r\n\r\n<p><code>Slave Node 192.168.2.52 and Node 192.168.2.53 on /etc/redis/redis.conf<br />\r\nbind 0.0.0.0 (or you can comment it for redis client to connect)<br />\r\nprotected-mode no<br />\r\nport 6379<br />\r\ndaemonized yes<br />\r\nsupervised systemd<br />\r\nslaveof 192.168.2.51 6379<br />\r\nmasterauth ******<br />\r\nrequirepass ******</code></p>\r\n\r\n<p><strong>Configure Redis Sentinel</strong></p>\r\n\r\n<p>Lets configure the <strong>Redis</strong> <strong>Sentinel</strong> on all the <strong>Nodes 192.168.2.51, 192.168.2.52 and 192.168.2.53 </strong>on <strong>/etc/redis/sentinel.conf</strong><br />\r\n<code>protected-mode yes<br />\r\nport 26379<br />\r\ndaemonize yes<br />\r\nsupervised systemd<br />\r\nsentinel auth-pass mymaster ******<br />\r\nsentinel down-after-milliseconds mymaster 5000<br />\r\nsentinel failover-timeout mymaster 60000<br />\r\npidfile &quot;/run/sentinel/redis-sentinel.pid&quot;<br />\r\nloglevel notice<br />\r\nlogfile &quot;/var/log/redis/redis-sentinel.log&quot;<br />\r\ndir &quot;/var/lib/redis&quot;<br />\r\nsentinel monitor mymaster 192.168.2.51 6379 2<br />\r\nacllog-max-len 128<br />\r\nsentinel deny-scripts-reconfig yes<br />\r\nsentinel resolve-hostnames no<br />\r\nsentinel announce-hostnames no<br />\r\nlatency-tracking-info-percentiles 50 99 99.9<br />\r\nuser default on nopass sanitize-payload ~* &amp;* +@all<br />\r\nsentinel myid b2619b3f4d652c6396cbecdb6ae67d413224c1bf<br />\r\nsentinel config-epoch mymaster 70<br />\r\nsentinel leader-epoch mymaster 70<br />\r\nsentinel current-epoch 70</code></p>\r\n\r\n<p>Restart Redis Server and Redis Sentinel Services on all 3 Nodes</p>\r\n\r\n<ul>\r\n	<li><strong>sudo systemctl restart redis-server.service</strong></li>\r\n	<li><strong>sudo systemctl restart redis-sentinel.service</strong></li>\r\n</ul>\r\n\r\n<p>This should update the service with the new configurations.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Command to test ports &mdash;<strong> sudo netstat -tulpn | grep -i redis</strong></p>\r\n\r\n<p><img alt=\"\" src=\"https://miro.medium.com/v2/resize:fit:700/1*g1xZd6RDtjYXWZQaEFwYow.png\" style=\"height:33px; width:700px\" /></p>\r\n\r\n<p>Test to see if Redis Server and Sentinel ports are open for connections</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Master Node logs should look something like this -</p>\r\n\r\n<p><img alt=\"\" src=\"https://miro.medium.com/v2/resize:fit:700/1*UqUvhODonbUvdvMu0_RT-w.png\" style=\"height:184px; width:700px\" /></p>\r\n\r\n<p>Master Node logs</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Check the master node using the following<br />\r\n- <strong>redis-cli -p 26379 sentinel get-master-addr-by-name </strong></p>\r\n\r\n<p>Output should have <strong>192.168.2.51 </strong>and<strong> port 6379</strong></p>\r\n\r\n<p><strong>Failover Testing</strong></p>\r\n\r\n<p>Bring down the master node by using the following<br />\r\n<strong>- sudo systemctl stop redis-server.service (or pause the VM)</strong></p>\r\n\r\n<p>Check the new master node<br />\r\n- <strong>redis-cli -p 26379 sentinel get-master-addr-by-name </strong></p>\r\n\r\n<p>Either 192.168.2.52 or 192.168.2.53 should be promoted as Master</p>\r\n\r\n<p>This ensures that the Redis cluster is always up.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\r\n\r\n<p><strong>HA Proxy</strong></p>\r\n\r\n<p>A load balancer/proxy is needed for clients to sconnect to because the client won&#39;t know which server is the master, HA Proxy in this case will tetermine which server has the tole of master and direc traffic to it over port 6379</p>\r\n\r\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\r\n\r\n<p>So without further adue her is the haproxy.cfg</p>\r\n\r\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\r\n\r\n<p><code>global<br />\r\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; log /dev/log&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; local0<br />\r\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; log /dev/log&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; local1 notice<br />\r\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; chroot /var/lib/haproxy<br />\r\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; stats socket /run/haproxy/admin.sock mode 660 level admin<br />\r\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; stats timeout 30s<br />\r\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; user haproxy<br />\r\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; group haproxy<br />\r\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; daemon</code></p>\r\n\r\n<p><code># Redis stuff<br />\r\nfrontend RedisSentinelGateway<br />\r\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; bind&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; 192.168.2.40:6379 name 192.168.2.40:6379<br />\r\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; mode&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; tcp<br />\r\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; log&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; global<br />\r\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; timeout client&nbsp; 30000<br />\r\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; default_backend RedisSentinelBackEnd</code></p>\r\n\r\n<p><code>backend RedisSentinelBackEnd<br />\r\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; mode&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; tcp<br />\r\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; timeout connect 30000<br />\r\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; timeout server&nbsp; 30000<br />\r\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; retries&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; 3<br />\r\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; option&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; tcp-check<br />\r\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; tcp-check&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; connect<br />\r\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; tcp-check&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; send AUTH ******<br />\r\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; tcp-check&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; send PING<br />\r\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; tcp-check&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; expect string +PONG<br />\r\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; tcp-check&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; send info replication<br />\r\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; tcp-check&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; expect string role:master<br />\r\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; tcp-check&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; send QUIT<br />\r\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; tcp-check&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; expect string +OK</code></p>\r\n\r\n<p><code>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; server&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Redis-Server-192-168-2-51 192.168.2.51:6379 check inter 1000 maxconn 1024<br />\r\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; server&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Redis-Server-192-168-2-52 192.168.2.52:6379 check inter 1000 maxconn 1024<br />\r\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; server&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Redis-Server-192-168-2-53 192.168.2.53:6379 check inter 1000 maxconn 1024</code></p>\r\n\r\n<p><code>frontend RedisSentinelStats<br />\r\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; mode&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; http<br />\r\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; bind&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; *:8404&nbsp; ssl crt /etc/ssl/fullchain.pem<br />\r\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; stats&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; enable<br />\r\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; stats&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; uri /<br />\r\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; stats&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; refresh 10s<br />\r\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; timeout connect 30000<br />\r\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; timeout server&nbsp; 30000</code></p>\r\n\r\n<p><code># /redis stuff</code></p>\r\n\r\n<p><code>defaults<br />\r\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; log&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; global<br />\r\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; mode&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; http<br />\r\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; option&nbsp; httplog<br />\r\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; option&nbsp; dontlognull<br />\r\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; timeout connect 5000<br />\r\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; timeout client&nbsp; 50000<br />\r\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; timeout server&nbsp; 50000<br />\r\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; errorfile 400 /etc/haproxy/errors/400.http<br />\r\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; errorfile 403 /etc/haproxy/errors/403.http<br />\r\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; errorfile 408 /etc/haproxy/errors/408.http<br />\r\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; errorfile 500 /etc/haproxy/errors/500.http<br />\r\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; errorfile 502 /etc/haproxy/errors/502.http<br />\r\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; errorfile 503 /etc/haproxy/errors/503.http<br />\r\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; errorfile 504 /etc/haproxy/errors/504.http</code></p>\r\n\r\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\r\n\r\n<p>So now if we point a browser to HAProxy</p>\r\n\r\n<p><img alt=\"\" src=\"/perl/images/ha1.png\" style=\"height:516px; width:1037px\" /></p>\r\n\r\n<p><img alt=\"\" src=\"/perl/images/kvm1.png\" /> <img alt=\"\" src=\"/perl/images/kvm2.png\" /></p>\r\n\r\n<p><img alt=\"\" src=\"/perl/images/ha2.png\" style=\"height:516px; width:1037px\" /></p>\r\n\r\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\r\n',	NULL,	NULL,	NULL),
(55,	1,	2,	1,	'Useful commands relating to openssl',	'',	'<p><strong>Generate csr and cert for use in redis</strong></p>\r\n\r\n<p>&nbsp; openssl req -new -x509 -days 365 -keyout ca.key -out ca.crt -subj &quot;/CN=Redis CA&quot;<br />\r\n&nbsp; openssl genrsa -out redis.key 2048<br />\r\n&nbsp; openssl req -new -key redis.key -out redis.csr -subj &quot;/CN=redis.example.com&quot;<br />\r\n&nbsp; openssl x509 -req -in redis.csr -CA ca.crt -CAkey ca.key -CAcreateserial -out redis.crt -days 365<br />\r\n<br />\r\n&nbsp;</p>\r\n\r\n<p><strong><span dir=\"ltr\">Verify the cert and key</span></strong></p>\r\n\r\n<p><span dir=\"ltr\">$ openssl rsa -modulus -noout -in /etc/ssl/redis/redis.key | openssl md5</span><br />\r\n<span dir=\"ltr\">$ openssl x509 -modulus -noout -in /etc/ssl/redis/redis.crt | openssl md5</span></p>\r\n\r\n<p><span dir=\"ltr\">both hash should match</span></p>\r\n\r\n<p><strong><span dir=\"ltr\">And then finaly:</span></strong></p>\r\n\r\n<p><span dir=\"ltr\">openssl verify -verbose -CAfile /etc/ssl/redis/ca.crt /etc/ssl/redis/redis.crt</span></p>\r\n\r\n<p><span dir=\"ltr\">should return OK</span></p>\r\n\r\n<p><strong>Generate cacert (BSD)</strong></p>\r\n\r\n<p><code>$ openssh genrsa -aes256 cacert.key 4096</code></p>\r\n\r\n<p><code>$ openssl req -x509 -new -nodes -key cacert.key -sha256 -days 365 -out cacert.pem</code></p>\r\n\r\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\r\n\r\n<p><strong>Check the modulus on a cert</strong></p>\r\n\r\n<p>$ openssl req -noout -modulus -in $csr</p>\r\n\r\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\r\n',	NULL,	NULL,	NULL),
(56,	1,	2,	1,	'Activate TLS on a redis sentinel setup',	'',	'<p>This is how to get Redis, Redis Sentinel operatin with TLS</p>\r\n\r\n<p>If, like me you ned to use your own self signed certificates, that is possible as outlined below</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Create a folder to store them:</p>\r\n\r\n<p><code>mkdir RedisCerts<br />\r\ncd RedisCerts<br />\r\nopenssl req -new -x509 -days 365 -keyout ca.key -out ca.crt -subj &quot;/CN=Redis CA&quot;<br />\r\n# Enter some passphrase for the key<br />\r\nopenssl genrsa -out redis.key 2048<br />\r\nopenssl req -new -key redis.key -out redis.csr -subj &quot;/CN=haproxy.dedicatedtoservers.com&quot;<br />\r\n# change the CN above to reflect your (sub)domain<br />\r\nopenssl x509 -req -in redis.csr -CA ca.crt -CAkey ca.key -CAcreateserial -out redis.crt -days 365<br />\r\nCertificate request self-signature ok<br />\r\nsubject=CN = haproxy.dedicatedtoservers.com<br />\r\nEnter pass phrase for ca.key:</code></p>\r\n\r\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\r\n\r\n<p><code>Now you have the cert, key and ca we check they are valis with the following bash script</code></p>\r\n\r\n<p>Create this script, savhe it a checkcert.sh and ensure it has the executable bit set</p>\r\n\r\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\r\n\r\n<p><code>#!/bin/bash<br />\r\nKEY=$1<br />\r\nCERT=$2<br />\r\nCA=$3</code></p>\r\n\r\n<p><code>if [[ $# != 3 ]]; then<br />\r\necho &quot;I expected 3 arguments&quot;<br />\r\necho &quot;[checkcert.sh KEY CERT CA]&quot;<br />\r\nexit<br />\r\nfi</code></p>\r\n\r\n<p><code>openssl rsa -modulus -noout -in $KEY | openssl md5<br />\r\nopenssl x509 -modulus -noout -in $CERT | openssl md5<br />\r\nopenssl verify -verbose -CAfile $CA $CERT</code><br />\r\n&nbsp;</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Now run the script and check the modulus on the key and cert is the same and the CA check returns &quot;OK&quot;</p>\r\n\r\n<p><code>./checkcert.sh redis.key redis.crt ca.crt<br />\r\nMD5(stdin)= dc6ec99338019fc59b8d2d26cc36e4bf<br />\r\nMD5(stdin)= dc6ec99338019fc59b8d2d26cc36e4bf<br />\r\nredis.crt: OK</code><br />\r\n&nbsp;</p>\r\n\r\n<p>So at this point you can use the above should you not have a signed certificate (tested and works fine for me)</p>\r\n\r\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\r\n\r\n<p><strong>Configure Redis</strong></p>\r\n\r\n<p>Add the following to /etc/redis.conf on each server</p>\r\n\r\n<p><code>port 6379<br />\r\ntls-port 8000<br />\r\ntls-cert-file &quot;/etc/ssl/redis/redis.crt&quot;<br />\r\ntls-key-file &quot;/etc/ssl/redis/redis.key&quot;<br />\r\ntls-ca-cert-file &quot;/etc/ssl/redis/ca.crt&quot;<br />\r\ntls-protocols &quot;TLSv1.2 TLSv1.3&quot;<br />\r\ntls-ciphersuites &quot;TLS_CHACHA20_POLY1305_SHA256&quot;</code></p>\r\n\r\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\r\n\r\n<p><code>Here we keep the standard port (3679) for haproxy health/availability checks to work and add port 8000 for TLS, tls-protocols is just to ensure at least TLS1_2</code></p>\r\n\r\n<p><code>tls-ciphersuites is (apparently) needed for TLS1_3 - you&#39;d change the key, cert and ca to reflect the location you saved them to</code><br />\r\n&nbsp;</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Finally restart the service</p>\r\n\r\n<p>sudo systemctl restart redis-server</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Do the above for each redis server</p>\r\n\r\n<p><strong>Configure Sentinel</strong></p>\r\n\r\n<p>This is very similar to the above but we use different ports and tell the sentinel to use TLS for replication</p>\r\n\r\n<p>port 26379<br />\r\ntls-port 36379<br />\r\ntls-replication yes<br />\r\ntls-cert-file &quot;/etc/ssl/redis/redis.crt&quot;<br />\r\ntls-key-file &quot;/etc/ssl/redis/redis.key&quot;<br />\r\ntls-ca-cert-file &quot;/etc/ssl/redis/ca.crt&quot;<br />\r\ntls-protocols &quot;TLSv1.2 TLSv1.3&quot;<br />\r\ntls-ciphersuites &quot;TLS_CHACHA20_POLY1305_SHA256&quot;<br />\r\n&nbsp;</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Finally restart the service</p>\r\n\r\n<p>sudo systemctl restart redis-sentinel</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Do the above for each redis sentinel</p>\r\n\r\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\r\n\r\n<p><strong>Now test it works</strong></p>\r\n\r\n<p>redis-cli &nbsp; -h haproxy.dedicatedtoservers.com &nbsp;-p 8000 &nbsp; &nbsp;--tls --cert /etc/ssl/redis/redis.crt &nbsp;--key /etc/ssl/redis/redis.key &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; --cacert /etc/ssl/redis/ca.crt &nbsp;-a info |grep role<br />\r\n<br />\r\n<strong>role:</strong>master<br />\r\n&nbsp;</p>\r\n\r\n<p>38:M 17 Oct 2024 09:38:55.110 * Delay next BGSAVE for diskless SYNC<br />\r\n738:M 17 Oct 2024 09:39:00.498 * Starting BGSAVE for SYNC with target: replicas sockets<br />\r\n738:M 17 Oct 2024 09:39:00.499 * Background RDB transfer started by pid 959<br />\r\n959:C 17 Oct 2024 09:39:00.501 * Fork CoW for RDB: current 0 MB, peak 0 MB, average 0 MB<br />\r\n738:M 17 Oct 2024 09:39:00.501 * Diskless rdb transfer, done reading from pipe, 1 replicas still up.<br />\r\n738:M 17 Oct 2024 09:39:00.539 * Background RDB transfer terminated with success<br />\r\n738:M 17 Oct 2024 09:39:00.539 * Streamed RDB transfer with replica 192.168.2.53:6379 succeeded (socket). Waiting for REPLCONF ACK from replica to enable streaming<br />\r\n738:M 17 Oct 2024 09:39:00.539 * Synchronization with replica 192.168.2.53:6379 succeeded</p>\r\n\r\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Now add som keys to the master</p>\r\n\r\n<p>127.0.0.1:6379&gt; SET &quot;MyNewKey&quot; &quot;MyNewValue&quot;<br />\r\nOK<br />\r\n127.0.0.1:6379&gt; keys *<br />\r\n1) &quot;MyNewKey&quot;<br />\r\n127.0.0.1:6379&gt;&nbsp;<br />\r\n&nbsp;</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Check the slaves</p>\r\n\r\n<p><img alt=\"\" src=\"/perl/images/Replication.png\" style=\"height:328px; width:900px\" /></p>\r\n',	NULL,	NULL,	NULL),
(57,	1,	1,	1,	'sed + remove comments and blank lines ',	'',	'<pre>\r\nsed -i &#39;s/#.*$//&#39;  fileName\r\nsed -i &#39;/^$/d&#39; fileName</pre>\r\n',	NULL,	NULL,	NULL),
